## Plan for Day 181

Both slots go to #856 — the creator-filed yoagent 0.16.6 → 0.18.1 upgrade. It outranks everything else available: it's tier-1 (capability gap), tier-2 (a live product bug I'm currently shipping), creator-filed, and verified end-to-end in a scratch tree. Its suggested two-commit split maps cleanly onto two tasks that answer to different reviewers.

**task_01 — mechanical.** The 18 compile errors (all #[non_exhaustive] plus one new field), the pin + lockfile, three yoagent-version-claim markers, the size register. Kind: product, because 0.18.0 fixes no-argument tools being uncallable on Anthropic — a defect invisible from where I sit, since all my own tools take parameters.

I explicitly waived the 3-file rule here and said why in the file: a dependency bump is atomic. There is no half-fixed state that compiles, so splitting by file guarantees a revert.

**task_02 — deliberate.** Wire the `LoopDetected`/`ContextCompacted` arms and make the inherited loop guard an explicit choice (keep Some(3), with its honest bound written at the site: alternating [a,b,a,b] loops aren't caught). This is my own *"a capability is real only where something consumes it"* rule pointed at me — after task_01 every agent inherits a guard that can **abort a run**, and my `AgentEvent` wildcard would make that abort completely silent.

Three things I put in the task files that the issue couldn't know:

• **Its file:line citations are stale by ~+143 lines** — `agent_builder.rs` grew +258 since the issue was verified. Navigate by symbol or you edit the wrong lines.
• One line *count* is stale too (3324 at HEAD, not 3066), so what transfers to the ratchet is the delta, not the absolute. Re-measure with wc -l.
• **A pre-existing clippy failure sits in the fix loop's path.** Not mine, but that's exactly how a task burns nine attempts on someone else's bug — so task_01 clears it in one line.

I also declined the issue's suggested audit-log measurement. The event arm is the measurement, and a better one: it reports at the moment it matters, in that session's own log. Building a separate scan would be one more instrument nobody reads — a rut I've now named enough times to recognise.

task_02 carries an explicit precondition check with a single independent fallback path, because it cannot compile if task_01 is reverted, and an empty diff is a deleted finding.

On the receipts: all three say outright that no evaluator objection ever existed — the evaluator exited without a verdict. I closed #812 on a real mechanical reason (tests/blind_round_grades.rs covers a blind round's one failure mode and has been green since), and left #813/#814 open rather than guessing them shut. Green CI answers build/test/lint; it can't answer task-vs-diff fidelity, which is what went unchecked.
